# Reading a null: power, precision, and the Oregon blood-sugar result
next: {"title":"Genes as instruments: Mendelian randomization","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/genes-as-instruments-mendelian-randomization.cast"}

## Absence of Evidence
voice: male
level: advanced
use: forest_plot
with:
    measure: MD
    null_line: 0
    title: Effect on elevated blood sugar (percentage points)
    studies:
      - {label: HbA1c ≥ 6.5% (Oregon), est: -0.93, lo: -2.47, hi: 0.61, weight: 8}
      - {label: A precise zero (hypothetical), est: -0.05, lo: -0.3, hi: 0.2, weight: 5}

Across these four parts we take apart one table — the most argued-over null result in health economics — and show that the whole answer lives in a standard error, not in a p-value.
    draw title axis

Everything hangs on this dashed line: zero, meaning insurance changed nothing.
    draw null_line

Two years after Oregon's lottery, the study brought in about twelve thousand people — twelve thousand two hundred, to be exact — and measured blood pressure, cholesterol, and glycated hemoglobin: blood sugar.
    draw study_label_0

Here is the blood-sugar result — for compliers, the LATE we built in lecture seven: the share above the diabetic threshold fell by about nine tenths of a percentage point.
    draw study_0

    pause 0.4

And here is its ninety-five percent confidence interval: from roughly minus two and a half points to plus six tenths.
    draw ci_0 est_label_0

It covers zero, so the p-value sits far above five percent — and blood pressure and cholesterol came back the same way: no stars, no significance.
    point at.ref null_line gesture tap

So the headline wrote itself: Medicaid buys care, but doesn't improve physical health. Hmm — well, that reading is what this whole series is about.
    delivery soft

Because two very different findings can both look like nothing.
    draw study_label_1 delivery brisk

Imagine a hypothetical study with the same near-zero estimate, but measured this tightly.
    draw study_1 ci_1 est_label_1

That bar says: whatever Medicaid does to blood sugar, it is at most a couple of tenths of a point. That is evidence of absence — an informative zero.
    focus study_1 ci_1 est_label_1 study_label_1

Oregon's bar says nothing of the sort. Its left end sits at minus two and a half percentage points — and only about five percent of the sample had elevated blood sugar to begin with.
    highlight ci_0 effect glow delivery grave

So this interval is compatible with Medicaid roughly halving the rate of elevated blood sugar, and equally compatible with it doing absolutely nothing.
    mark mark_ci target ci_0 kind box

    pause 0.5

When the results appeared in 2013, critics read the table as proof that Medicaid fails to improve physical health, while health economists — Austin Frakt and Aaron Carroll among the loudest — replied that the study was never precise enough to see a benefit worth having.
    highlight est_label_0 effect pulse

Neither side did the arithmetic wrong; they disagree about what an insignificant estimate licenses you to claim, and the two-year data simply cannot settle it.

Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence — and the difference between the two is the width of this bar, not the star beside the p-value.
    point at.ref ci_0 gesture underline delivery grave

Next time we stop asking whether the interval covers zero, and start asking what else it covers.

## The interval, not the star
voice: male
level: advanced
use: number_line
with:
    min: -0.5
    max: 0.5
    step: 0.25
    points:
      - {at: -0.01, label: LATE = −0.01}
      - {at: -0.3, label: 'A real gain: −0.3'}
    intervals:
      - {from: -0.09, to: 0.08, label: '95% CI: −0.09 to 0.08'}
    arrow_ops:
      - {from: 0, to: -0.05, label: Big effect for 1 in 20 → −0.05 on the mean}

Now that we have seen why a missing star is not evidence of absence, let's do the thing a p-value cannot: put Oregon's blood-sugar estimate on a line, in percentage points of glycated hemoglobin, and read off what it rules out.
    draw line tick_-0.5 tick_-0.25 tick_0 tick_0.25 tick_0.5

Here is the number — the effect of coverage on the compliers, the LATE from the lottery instrument: minus 0.01 percentage points. Essentially nothing.
    draw pt_0

    pause 0.3

And here is the part that actually carries information: the ninety-five percent interval, from minus 0.09 to plus 0.08.
    draw interval_0

Every value inside this band is one the data cannot reject; every value outside it, the data can.
    camera center.ref interval_0 zoom 2.2

The p-value told us only that zero sits somewhere in here — it never told us how narrow here is.
    point at.ref interval_0 gesture underline

So, to judge that band we need a benchmark: how large an effect would have been worth having?
    camera reset true

Metformin, the first-line diabetes drug, lowers glycated hemoglobin by roughly a full percentage point; even a third of a point — out here — would be a genuine clinical gain.
    draw pt_1

And that value lies far outside the interval. Oregon rules it out.
    mark cross_meaningful target pt_1 kind cross
    delivery grave

Which is a finding, not a failure — the study established that the average effect on blood sugar is smaller than a tenth of a point in either direction.
    highlight interval_0 effect glow

But hold on — smaller than a tenth of a point, averaged over whom?
    clear keep ["line","tick_-0.5","tick_-0.25","tick_0","tick_0.25","tick_0.5","pt_0","interval_0"]

The control group's average was about 5.17 percent. Most of these adults simply did not have diabetes; only around one in twenty had blood sugar in the diabetic range.
    highlight pt_0 effect glow

So suppose Medicaid gave that one in twenty a full point of improvement — a large clinical effect. Spread over everyone, the average moves five hundredths of a point. To here.
    draw hop_0

Which is comfortably inside the interval. A tight bound on the average is perfectly compatible with a big effect on the few people who were actually sick.
    point at.ref hop_0 gesture circle delivery grave

And this is precisely where the fight broke out: Baicker and her co-authors wrote that their intervals could not rule out clinically significant changes, while commentators read the same table as proof that Medicaid does not improve physical health — nobody disputing the number, everybody disputing which parameter it bounds.
    highlight interval_0 effect pulse delivery soft

And the mechanism was plainly moving: coverage significantly raised diabetes diagnoses and the use of diabetes medication, even as the average marker stood still.
    text note_dx "Medicaid did raise diabetes diagnosis and medication use" #2f6b8f x 500 y 140 font_size 24

Report the interval, not the star: Oregon ruled out large average improvements in blood sugar — it never ruled out a modest one, or a large one for the handful who had the disease.

